      "knowledge_point_id": "603badf5-1f56-581f-833a-ec08fbedf777",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Equation of a Circle Given a Line Covering a Diameter",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043932/assets/36e373800d2bd917e38bec197fbb5add2ae785270c68ddb4daf740ad678d4ece.png)\n\nThe line $x+y=2$ intercepts the $x$- and $y$-axes at points $A$ and $B$, respectively. If the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, find the equation of the circle.",
      "worked_solution": "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+0 & =2 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+y & =2 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the  coordinates $(2,0)$ and $(0,2)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{2+0}{2},\\frac{0+2}{2})=(1,1).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(1,1)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(2,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{(1-2)^{2}+(1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(1,1)$ and radius $r=\\sqrt{2},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{2})}^{2} \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
